{"id": "prc:savasana", "name": "Śavāsana (the corpse; mṛtāsana)", "category": "posture", "method_summary": "Lying flat on the back like a corpse; the text says it removes fatigue and rests the mind (HYP 1.32).", "lineages": ["lin:hatha-yoga"], "sources": [{"source": "src:hatha-yoga-pradipika", "ref": "1.32", "rests_on": ["tea:hatha-yoga-pradipika:1.32"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": [], "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "prc:siddhasana", "name": "Siddhāsana (the adept's seat)", "category": "posture", "method_summary": "The best of the four chief postures: a seated posture with a heel at the base of the trunk, chin steady on the chest and the gaze fixed between the brows (HYP 1.35-40); the text says it opens the door of liberation and calls it also vajrāsana, muktāsana or guptāsana (1.37).", "lineages": ["lin:hatha-yoga"], "sources": [{"source": "src:hatha-yoga-pradipika", "ref": "1.34-1.39", "rests_on": ["tea:hatha-yoga-pradipika:1.34", "tea:hatha-yoga-pradipika:1.35", "tea:hatha-yoga-pradipika:1.36", "tea:hatha-yoga-pradipika:1.37", "tea:hatha-yoga-pradipika:1.38", "tea:hatha-yoga-pradipika:1.39"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": [], "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "prc:padmasana", "name": "Padmāsana (the lotus seat)", "category": "posture", "method_summary": "The lotus posture, feet on opposite thighs (HYP 1.44-47); the text gives two descriptions.", "lineages": ["lin:hatha-yoga"], "sources": [{"source": "src:hatha-yoga-pradipika", "ref": "1.44-1.47", "rests_on": ["tea:hatha-yoga-pradipika:1.44", "tea:hatha-yoga-pradipika:1.45", "tea:hatha-yoga-pradipika:1.47"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": [], "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "prc:simhasana", "name": "Siṃhāsana (the lion seat)", "category": "posture", "method_summary": "The lion posture, ankles under the perineum, hands on the knees, mouth open, gaze on the nose-tip (HYP 1.50-51).", "lineages": ["lin:hatha-yoga"], "sources": [{"source": "src:hatha-yoga-pradipika", "ref": "1.50-1.51", "rests_on": ["tea:hatha-yoga-pradipika:1.50", "tea:hatha-yoga-pradipika:1.51"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": [], "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "prc:bhadrasana", "name": "Bhadrāsana (the auspicious seat)", "category": "posture", "method_summary": "The bhadra posture, ankles under the perineum, sides of the feet held; also called gorakṣāsana (HYP 1.53-54).", "lineages": ["lin:hatha-yoga"], "sources": [{"source": "src:hatha-yoga-pradipika", "ref": "1.53-1.54", "rests_on": ["tea:hatha-yoga-pradipika:1.53", "tea:hatha-yoga-pradipika:1.54"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": [], "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "prc:nadanusandhana", "name": "Attention to the inner sound (nādānusandhāna) in the haṭha texts", "category": "mantra-sound", "method_summary": "Attention to the inner sound (nāda): seated, with the ears closed by the hands, the mind is fixed on the sound heard, and on the subtler sound as it appears, until it dissolves in it (HYP 4.65-68, 4.82-99); the text says it is accepted even for the dull (4.65) and calls it the chief of the ways of laya (4.66). The instruction of 4.68 that touches restricted practice is not reproduced.", "lineages": ["lin:hatha-yoga"], "sources": [{"source": "src:hatha-yoga-pradipika", "ref": "4.65-4.93", "rests_on": ["tea:hatha-yoga-pradipika:4.65", "tea:hatha-yoga-pradipika:4.66", "tea:hatha-yoga-pradipika:4.67", "tea:hatha-yoga-pradipika:4.68", "tea:hatha-yoga-pradipika:4.82", "tea:hatha-yoga-pradipika:4.83", "tea:hatha-yoga-pradipika:4.87", "tea:hatha-yoga-pradipika:4.88", "tea:hatha-yoga-pradipika:4.89", "tea:hatha-yoga-pradipika:4.93"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": [], "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "prc:hatha-yama-niyama", "name": "The restraints and observances of the haṭha texts", "category": "ethics", "method_summary": "The bracketed list in the e-text of HYP 1.17: ten yamas and ten niyamas; the list appears to be inserted and is not consistent with 1.38.", "lineages": ["lin:hatha-yoga"], "sources": [{"source": "src:hatha-yoga-pradipika", "ref": "1.17-1.38", "rests_on": ["tea:hatha-yoga-pradipika:1.17", "tea:hatha-yoga-pradipika:1.38"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": [], "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
